/*
⏱️ Time Complexity

O(rows × cols)

Each cell is visited once.

DFS does not re-visit cells because you mark them as '0'.

💾 Space Complexity

Auxiliary space: O(rows × cols) in the worst case (DFS recursion stack if the grid is all land)

Extra data structures: O(1) (grid is modified in-place)

✔️ If using BFS, it would be the same time but queue space instead of recursion stack.

🧠 Simple intuition

Traverse the grid cell by cell.

When you find land ('1'), you’ve discovered a new island.

Use DFS to sink the entire island (turn connected '1's into '0's).

Continue scanning — any remaining '1' must belong to a different island.

Think of it as:

“Whenever I see land, I flood-fill it and count it once.”
*/
class Solution {
    int rows,cols;
    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {
        int count = 0;
        rows = grid.length;
        cols = grid[0].length;
        for(int i=0;i<rows;i++){
            for(int j=0;j<cols;j++){
                if(grid[i][j]=='1'){
                    count++;
                    dfs(grid,i,j);
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
    public void dfs(char[][] grid,int r,int c){
        if(r<0 || c<0 || r>=rows || c>=cols || grid[r][c]=='0'){
            return;
        }
        grid[r][c]='0';
        dfs(grid,r+1,c);
        dfs(grid,r-1,c);
        dfs(grid,r,c+1);
        dfs(grid,r,c-1);
    }
}
